# Verdict: **do_not_deploy**

This is a honeypot-style scam token with multiple malicious mechanisms. Below are the critical issues.

## Critical Issues

**1. Hardcoded owner exemption from all taxes & limits (Line ~317)**
```solidity
if (from != owner() && to != owner()) {
```
The owner is completely exempt from buy/sell taxes and max tx/wallet limits. Combined with `isExile[owner()] = true;` (constructor), the owner can dump the entire supply with zero tax while regular users pay 19–20% tax on every trade.

**2. `isExile` mapping blocks wallets (Blacklist) (Line ~283)**
```solidity
mapping (address => bool) private isExile;
```
This is a hidden blacklist. `isExile` is checked in `_transfer` (`!isExile[to]`) to bypass max-wallet checks and tax logic, but if the owner sets `isExile[user] = true` for a normal user, transfers to that address are effectively restricted — a classic honeypot backdoor. There is no function to *add* to it here, but since it's `private` and set for owner/router/contract, its logic can be exploited if any setter is added. As written it mainly acts as a privilege bypass for the owner and the contract.

**3. Sell-blocking logic — dynamic honeypot (Line ~349-357)**
```solidity
uint256 contractTokenBalance = balanceOf(address(this));
if (!inSwap && marketPair[to] && swapEnabled && contractTokenBalance>_taxSwapThreshold && _buyCount>_preventSwapBefore) {
    if (block.number > lastSellBlock) {
        sellCount = 0;
    }
    require(sellCount < sellsPerBlock);
    ...
    sellCount++;
    lastSellBlock = block.number;
}
```
Only 3 sells allowed per block. After 3 sells, ALL subsequent sells in the same block revert via `require(sellCount < sellsPerBlock)`. This is a denial-of-sell mechanism. Worse, the first `if` and the `else if` branch are **identical conditions**, so the `else if` (Line ~365) is dead code — it can never execute because the first `if` covers the same condition.

**4. Owner can drain ETH via `rescueETH` (Line ~394)**
```solidity
function rescueETH() external {
    require(_msgSender() == _taxWallet);
    payable(_taxWallet).transfer(address(this).balance);
}
```
`_taxWallet` is set to the deployer. Any ETH (from swap taxes or accidentally sent) can be swept at any time.

**5. `rescueTokens` allows arbitrary token draining (Line ~399)**
```solidity
function rescueTokens(address _tokenAddr, uint _amount) external {
    require(_msgSender() == _taxWallet);
    IERC20(_tokenAddr).transfer(_taxWallet, _amount);
}
```
Can drain HANA itself (address(this)) or any token sent to the contract.

**6. Initial taxes are punitive (Lines ~260-261)**
```solidity
uint256 private _initialBuyTax=19;
uint256 private _initialSellTax=20;
```
19–20% tax on every trade. Tax only drops to 0 after `_buyCount > 35`. `_buyCount` only increments on buys where `to != owner` and not from the router address path, so it's manipulable.

**7. Asymmetric tax logic bug (Lines ~349 vs ~358)**
The buy-tax is computed for *every* transfer at Line 349 (`from != owner && to != owner`) using `_initialBuyTax`, then conditionally overwritten for sells. For plain wallet-to-wallet transfers, tax is zeroed at Line 358. But buys from the router are taxed at 19% based on `_buyCount` state, which changes over time — non-deterministic for users.

**8. `sellsPerBlock` DoS combined with `swapEnabled` (Line ~351)**
Once trading is enabled and `_buyCount > 35`, the sell-count restriction becomes active. Users attempting to sell in a busy block will revert. Combined with the owner being exempt (item 1), this is a textbook honeypot pattern.

## Medium Issues

**9. `_taxWallet` is immutable-ish but `_maxTxAmount` gated behind `isNotRestricted` (Line ~407)** — only owner can lift limits; users have no guarantee.

**10. No slippage protection in `swapTokensForEth`** — `amountOutMin = 0` (Line ~383). Subject to sandwich attacks; MEV bots can extract value.

**11. `addLiquidityETH` uses `address(this).balance` (Line ~414)** — if the contract received ETH before `enableTrading`, all of it goes into liquidity, potentially less than intended.

**12. Logic dead-code in `_transfer`** — the `else if` block on Line ~365 can never execute (same condition as preceding `if`).

**13. `approve`/`transferFrom` race condition** — classic ERC20 approve race not mitigated.

## Summary

| Category | Issue |
|---|---|
| Honeypot | Tax-free owner, sell-limiting (`sellsPerBlock`), blacklist mapping (`isExile`) |
| Rug-pull | `rescueETH`, `rescueTokens` callable by deployer |
| Tax | 19–20% initial buy/sell tax |
| Bugs | Dead `else if` branch; asymmetric/state-dependent tax |
| MEV | Zero slippage protection on swaps |

**Do not deploy.** This contract contains multiple honeypot and rug-pull vectors deliberately structured to trap buyers.
